\section{ Construction and Verification}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
Let $(A,M)$ be a simple polarized abelian surface of type $(1,2)$.
Assume furthermore that $M$ is symmetric,
i.e. $(-1)^*M \cong M$. The linear series $|M|$ has $4$ isolated
base points $\{o,p,q,r\}$. We may assume that $o$
is the identity of the abelian surface and $p,q,r$ are
$2$-torsion with $r=p+q$ (see, e.g. \cite{Ba}).
Each divisor $D \in |M|$ is either a nonsingular curve of genus $3$
or a singular curve with a simple node
distinct from the base points.

Let $\hat{A}$ be the dual abelian variety of $A$,
$\PP$ the Poincar\'e line bundle on $A\times \hat {A}$
and let $$\FF=\widehat {M^\vee }:=R^2p_{\hat{A}, *}(p_A^* M^\vee \ot \PP )$$
be the Fourier-Mukai transform
of $M^\vee$ (cf. \cite{Muk}).
The vector bundle $\FF$ has rank 2.
%and by \cite{Muk} it satisfies the WIT with index $0$
%(in other words $R^ip_{A,*}(p^*_{\hat{A}}\FF \ot \PP)=0$ for $i=1,2$).
%By cohomology and base change, one sees that $\FF$ satisfies the IT
%and in particular that $h^0(\hat{A},\FF)=1$, $h^i(\hat{A},\FF)= 0$ for $i>0$.
Let $\EE =\FF ^\vee$.
One can check that,
$$\dim \Hom (S^3 \EE, \Lambda ^2 \EE )=
h^0(\hat{A}, (S^3\EE)^\vee \ot \wedge ^2 \EE ) =2.$$
According to Miranda's triple covering construction \cite{Mi},
there is a $2$-dimensional family of triple coverings
$\hat{f}:\hat{X} \to \hat{A}$ with
$\hat{f}_* \OO_{\hat{X}}=\OO_{\hat{A}} \oplus \EE$.


The idea is as follows:
in order to construct a triple covering 
$\hat{f}: \hat{X} \lra \hat{A}$ over $\hat{A}$ with
{\it Tschirnhausen module} $\EE$ (cf. \cite{Mi}), we first construct a 
triple covering $f:X \lra A$ with
Tschirnhausen module $\phi_{M^\vee}^*\EE$. In               
Claim 1, we identify those that
descend to a triple covering $f:X \lra A$. In Claim 2,
we then verify that for a general such covering, the 
singularities of $X$ are
rational. It follows that
the singularities of $\hat{X}$ are also rational. Finally, 
one can compute the invariants
of $\hat{X}$ via invariants of $X$.

Let $\phi_{M^\vee } : A \lra \hat{A}$ be the isogeny defined by $M^\vee $.
We have the following commutative diagram:
$$
\begin{CD}
X @>{\phi}>> \hat{X} \\
@V{f}VV      @V{\hat{f}}VV \\
A @>{\phi_{M^\vee }}>> \hat{A}
\end{CD}
$$
Where $\phi: X \to \hat{X}$ is a $4:1$ etale covering and $f: X \to A$
is a triple covering
determined by a section of
$$\phi_{M^\vee }^* \Hom(S^3\EE ,\wedge^2 \EE )
\subset \Hom (S^3 \phi_{M^\vee }^*\EE , \wedge^2 \phi_{M^\vee}^*\EE ).$$

By \cite{Muk}, $\phi_{M^\vee }^*\EE \cong M^\vee \oplus M^\vee $. Thus
$$ \Hom (S^3 \phi_{M^\vee }
^*\EE , \wedge^2 \phi_{M^\vee}^*\EE ) \cong H^0(A, M)^{\oplus 4}.$$



In order to determine the corresponding
$2$-dimensional subspace, we consider the
Heisenberg group action on $H^0(A,M)$.
The Heisenberg group can be identified as
$$\mathcal{G}(\delta):= \{(\alpha,t,l)|
\alpha \in k^*, t \in \ZZ_2, l \in \hat{\ZZ_2} \}$$
with group law $(\alpha,t,l)(\alpha,t',l')=(\alpha \alpha' l'(t),t+t',l+l')$.
Moreover, $H^0(A,M)$ corresponds to $\Hom(\ZZ_2,k)$.
The action of $\mathcal{G}(\delta)$ on $\Hom(\ZZ_2,k)$
is given as $(\alpha,t,l)f(x)=\alpha l(x) f(t+x)$.
Let $X,Y$ be the sections in $H^0(A,M)$ corresponding to the characteristic
functions of $0,1$ in $\Hom(\ZZ_2,k)$ respectively.

{\bf Claim 1:} The $2$-dimensional subspace is determined as
$$\phi_{M^\vee}^* \Hom(S^3\EE,\wedge^2 \EE)
\cong \{(sX,tY,-tX,-sY)|s,t \in k\}\subset
H^0(A, M)^{\oplus 4}.$$

Grant the claim for the time being. Following Miranda (cf. \cite {Mi})
we can then construct a triple covering $f: X \to A$
by using the data $a=sX,b=tY,c=-tX,d=-sY$.
Over an affine open subset $U$ of $A$,
the triple covering can be described in
$U \times \AAA^2$ as
by the $2\times 2$ minors of
$$
\pmatrix Z+a & W-2d & c\\
b & Z-2a & W+d
\endpmatrix
$$
where $Z,W$ are coordinates for $\AAA^2$.

Following \cite{Mi} \S 4, we have $A=s^2X^2+stY^2$, $B=(t^2-s^2)XY$ and
$C=s^2Y^2+stX^2$. The branch locus is defined by $D=B^2-4AC \in H^0(M)^{\ot 4}$
and one can see that it corresponds to a divisor
$D_1+D_2+D_3+D_4$ with $D_i \in |M|$.
For general choice of $s,t$, the $D_i$ are all distinct and nonsingular.
It is easy to check (cf. \cite{Mi} \S 5)
that for general choices of $s,t$, the  only possible
singularities of $X$
lie over the $4$ base points $\{p,q,r,o\}$.
We remark that $f$ is totally ramified only over these $4$ base points.

Let $x \in X$ be a point lying over one of $\{p,q,r,o\}$.

{\bf Claim 2:} For general $s,t$, the singularity of $X$ at $x$,
is locally isomorphic to a cone over a twisted cubic.

Therefore, $X$ has only rational singularities.
A resolution $\mu: \tilde{X} \to X$ can be obtained by
blowing up along these $4$ points.
Let $\{E_i\}_{i=1,..,4}$ be the
exceptional divisors and $\{R_i\}_{i=1,...,4}$ be the proper transform
of the $D_i$, then
$$K_{\tilde{X}}= \sum_{i=1,...,4}R_i+\sum_{i=1,...,4}E_i.$$
Note that $R_i R_j=0$, $R_i E_j=1$ for all $i,j$ and $E_i^2=-3$,
$E_iE_j=0$ for $i \ne j$.
Thus we have $K_X^2=20$.
$$p_g(\tilde{X})=h^0(\tilde{X},\omega_{\tilde{X}})=
h^2(\tilde{X},  \OO _{\tilde {X}})=h^2(X,\OO_X)=$$
$$\hskip2cm h^2(A,\OO_A)+2h^2(A,M^{\vee })=5.$$
Similarly, $q(\tilde{X})=2$ and
$\chi(\tilde{X}, \omega_{\tilde{X}})=4$.
One can also check that $K_{\tilde{X}}$ is ample.
%Suppose there is an irreducible curve $C$ with $C K_X < 0$,
%then $C$ is a component of $K_X$ which is impossible.

Since $\phi: X \to \hat{X}$ is an etale cover of degree $4$,
one has a resolution $\hat{\mu}: {\hat{X}}^{\prime }\to \hat{X}$
such that $\tilde{X} \to {\hat{X}}^\prime $ is an etale cover of degree $4$.
Therefore $\chi({\hat{X}}^\prime ,\omega_{ {\hat{X}} ^\prime } )=1$.
It follows that $p_g({\hat{X}}^\prime )=2$, $K_{{\hat{X}}^\prime }$ is ample
and $(K_{{\hat{X}}^\prime })^2=5$.

\begin{proof} (Claim 1)
We follow \cite{Mum}. Let $H(M^\vee )$ be the kernel of $\phi _{M^\vee}:
A\lra \hat {A}$, i.e. the set of points $x\in A$ such that $T_x^*M^\vee
\cong M^\vee$. Then $H(M)=H(M^ \vee)$.
Let $\GG (M )$ be the set of pairs $(x, \varphi )$
such that $x\in H(M)$ and $\varphi$ is an isomorphism
$\varphi : M\lra T_x^*M$. Then $\GG (M )$ is a group
sitting in the following exact sequence:
$$0\lra k^* \lra \GG (M )\lra H(M )\lra 0.$$
There is an isomorphism of groups $\GG (M )\cong \GG (\delta )$.
Under this identification, the representation of $\GG (M )$ on
$H^0(A,M )$ corresponds to the unique representation of
$\GG (\delta )$ on $V=V(\delta):= \Hom (\ZZ _2,k)$, which is defined
by $((\alpha ,t,l)f)(x)=\alpha \cdot l(x)\cdot f(t+x)$.
With respect to the ordered basis $\{ \chi ^0, \chi ^1 \} $ (characteristic
functions of $0,1$ respectively), this representation is induced
by
$$(1,1,1)\rightarrow \left( \matrix 0 & 1 \\ -1 & 0 \endmatrix \right),
\hskip.25cm
(1,1,0)\rightarrow \left( \matrix 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \endmatrix \right),
\hskip.25cm
(1,0,1)\rightarrow \left( \matrix 1 & 0 \\ 0 & -1 \endmatrix \right).$$
The corresponding $\GG (\delta )$ representation on $S^3V^\vee \ot \Lambda
^2 V \ot V$ can easily be computed.
%Let $\rho,\mu \in D_4$ be generators
%such that $\rho ^4=\mu ^2=e$ and $\rho \mu =\mu \rho ^3$.
%With respect to the ordered basis $\{ \chi ^0, \chi ^1 \} $ (characteristic
%functions of $0,1$ respectively) of $V(\delta):=\Hom
%(\ZZ _2,k)$, there is a representation of $D_4$ such that
%$$\rho =\left( \matrix 0 & 1 \\ -1 & 0 \endmatrix \right)\hskip1cm
%\mu =\left( \matrix 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \endmatrix \right).$$
%The dual representation can easily be computed.
By \cite{Muk} Proposition 3.11, we have
$$\phi _{M^\vee }^* \FF  \cong
H^2(A,M)\ot M \cong (H^0(A,M)\ot M^\vee )^\vee \cong \phi _{M^\vee }
^* \EE ^\vee.$$
One sees that
$$H^0(A,S^3\phi _{M^\vee }^* \EE ^\vee \ot \Lambda ^2 \phi _{M^\vee }
^* \EE  ) \cong
S^3 H^0(A,M) ^\vee \ot \Lambda ^2 H^0(A,M ) \ot H^0(A,M) .$$
This vector space is in turn isomorphic to $\bigoplus _{i=1}^4 H^0(A,M)$.
We can now compute the corresponding $\GG (M )$ representation
in terms of the above $\GG (\delta )$ representation.

Let $p_i, i=1,...,4$ denote the projection onto the $i$-th
factor.
With respect to the ordered basis
$$\{e_1,e_2, ...,e_8\}=\{ p_1^*X,p_1^*Y,...,p_4^*X,p_4^* Y\}=$$
$$\{ \hat {X}^3\ot X\wedge Y\ot X, \hat {X}^3\ot X\wedge Y\ot Y,
\hat {X}^2\hat{Y}\ot X\wedge Y\ot X, \hat {X}^2\hat{Y}\ot X\wedge Y\ot Y,$$
$$\hat {X}\hat{Y}^2\ot X\wedge Y\ot X, \hat {X}\hat{Y}^2\ot X\wedge Y\ot Y,
\hat{Y}^3\ot X\wedge Y\ot X, \hat{Y}^3\ot X\wedge Y\ot Y\} ,$$
we have that $ (1,1,1)\rightarrow
R\in M_8(k)$ with $R_{i,j}=0$ if $i+j\ne 8$ and
$R_{i,8-i}=\{-1,1,1,-1,-1,1,1,-1 \}$, respectively
$(1,1,0)\rightarrow M\in M_8(k)$
with $M_{i,j}=0$ if $i+j\ne 8$ and
$M_{i,8-i}=\{1,1,1,1,1,1,1,1 \}$. In particular $R^2=M^2=1$ and
$RM=MR$. There is an induced representation of
$H(M^\vee )\cong \ZZ _2\times \ZZ _2 $.
It is easy to see that the $\ZZ _2\times \ZZ _2$
invariant elements are just the subspace
$$\{ s(e_1-e_8)+t(e_4-e_5)| s,t\in k\}.$$
These invariant elements correspond to the subspace $\phi _{M^\vee }^*
\Hom (S^3 \EE, \Lambda ^2 \EE)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof} (Claim 2) On a neighborhood of one of the base loci $o,p,q,r$
we may assume that $X,Y$ (or any two distinct sections of
$H^0(A,M)$) are local coordinates. By \cite{Ha} pg. 14 exercise 1.25,
the above mentioned $2\times 2$ minors define a twisted cubic if
and only if for all
$[u:v]\in \PPP ^1$, the linear forms
$$u(Z+sX)-vtY,u(W+2sY)-v(Z-2sX),
-utX-v(W-sY)$$ are linearly independent. In other words, if and only if
the matrix
$$
\pmatrix us & -vt & u & 0\\
2vs & 2us & -v & u \\
-ut & vs & 0 & -v
\endpmatrix
$$
has a nonzero $3\times 3$ minor for every value of $u,v$
By inspection one sees that this is the case for general $s,t$ (more precisely
for $t\ne 0$ and $t^2\ne 9s^2$).
\end{proof}


